 "turn_2": {
  "type": "answer",
  "content": "CHG-1 caused the issue. Do not roll back CHG-2.\n\n**Mechanism and Reason:**\nThe issue is memory contention caused by the reduced DRAM available for tiered VMs, not the addition of new VMs alone.\n\n1. **The Trigger (CHG-1):** On Tuesday, CHG-1 disabled memory tiering and added full memory reservations for six SQL VMs (256 GB each). According to `vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`, each full reservation shrinks the DRAM left for tiered VMs. This reduced the effective DRAM:NVMe ratio for the remaining tiered VMs.\n2. **The Saturation (CHG-2):** CHG-2 added 40 VMs. While this increased demand, the critical signal is that DRAM consumption passed the 80% threshold on Friday (`vsphere/technology/memory/concepts/memory-tiering`), which is when tiering actually starts moving pages to NVMe.\n3. **The Contention:** By Tuesday, the worst host's Active Memory reached 77% of DRAM. Per `vsphere/technology/memory/concepts/memory-tiering`, values above 75% result in significant performance loss. The \"warm-up\" reads (380 MB/s host total, 6 MB/s for `pay-api-07`) are fetching pages from NVMe because the working set no longer fits in the remaining DRAM.\n4. **The Overcommit:** The Highest ESXi Memory Overcommit Ratio rose to 1.23 on Thursday, shortly after CHG-2, but the performance drop only occurred when the Active working set exceeded the remaining DRAM on Tuesday morning.\n\n**What to check next:**\nCompare the current NVMe read bandwidth against the same time window from the previous long weekend to confirm if the working set now permanently exceeds the reduced DRAM capacity (`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`).",
